{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794757792,"nonce":"HknfnJv/FlsYpA87pPpjl7dQj5o=","title":"blinc_gpu: the particle_simulation golden on this machine does not match the russl shader, and the test is skipped when blinc_app is not in the build","message":"The golden `.blinc/snapshots/blinc_gpu/macos/particle_simulation.png` was written at 23:17 on 2026-11-10; the russl port of the particle shaders is ce5d0404 at 23:24 the same night. `particles_parity::the_simulation_matches_this_machines_golden` fails against it with 41 of 1024 pixels differing, at most 15 levels. The output at ce5d0404 and at HEAD is byte-identical (checked by blessing into a temp directory at each, same md5), and it is deterministic across runs, so no change since the port moved it; the golden is older than the shader it guards, or came from an earlier iteration of the test. The CPU-reference simulation tests in the same file pass.\n\nTwo things to decide.\n- Re-bless the golden (BLINC_SNAPSHOT=bless), or keep it and find out whether the port really differs from the hand-written WGSL by more than the commit message says (\"agreed to the bit\").\n- `blinc_gpu` built alone has no backend feature, so the test finds no adapter and passes without comparing anything. It compares only when `blinc_app` (through `blinc_gpu/desktop`) is in the build, which is how it came to fail in a multi-package nextest run and pass alone.\n\nAlso seen, not defects: `shaders::tests::sdf_shadow_hlsl_compiles_with_dxc` is #[ignore]d for lack of dxc and fails under --run-ignored all on this Mac (CI runs it on Windows); `bc4_quality::bc4_indices_are_the_nearest_palette_entry` is an #[ignore]d canary for a defect in the tbc crate and fails by design.\n\nLabels: debt, area:gpu.","files":null}]}